\section{The types}{

\itemize{
\item \code{"underline"} draws a line under the tag's words. It takes no room, so nothing moves. The line follows the tip
preset in use (see \code{\link[=tip_style]{tip_style()}}): dotted for \code{"none"}, \code{"sober"} and \code{"dense"}, dashed for \code{"modern"} and
\code{"editorial"}, and a long gentle wave for \code{"lively"}. Choose one with \code{line}.
\item \code{"icon"} adds a small icon after the words: a circle with an \code{i}, or the icon you give. It takes a little room.
\item \code{"corner"} draws a small triangle in the top corner of the tag, as a spreadsheet does for a note. It takes no room
and suits a table cell or a card; on a single word it can look like a stray mark, so prefer \code{"underline"} there.
\item \code{"none"} draws nothing. It is what a tag has when \code{marker} is left out.
}

A marker is drawn in the tag's own text colour, so it keeps its contrast in light and dark. The pointer over a marked
tag is the help cursor, unless the tip can be pinned (it is then a hand), or the tag is a button or a link, which
keep their own. A tag that takes more than words, such as the \code{div} Shiny puts around an input, is marked
as a whole: put the marker on the label tag, or on the words, when that is what you mean.
}

\section{Words that wrap}{

For an underline, the words of a block tag (a \code{td}, a \code{p}, a \code{div} and so on) are put in a \code{span} inside the tag, so
that the line goes under the words and not across the whole cell or paragraph. In a \code{\link[bslib:value_box]{bslib::value_box()}} the title
is marked. Tags inside the block that are not words are left as they are. A block that holds no words of its own,
such as a \code{div} of other tags, has nothing to underline and stops with an error: put the marker on the tag that holds
the words, or use \code{"corner"} or \code{"icon"}.
}

